<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Ribagorza</h1><p class="st-meta">872 CE – 1044 CE (approx.) · capital Roda de Isábena</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pyrenean county between Aragon and Catalonia, with its bishopric at Roda de Isábena, later absorbed into the Kingdom of Aragon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabena">Isábena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noguera-ribagorzana">Noguera Ribagorzana</a>, was part of the Frankish <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse</a> until around 872, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-i-of-pallars-and-ribagorza">Ramon I</a> established himself as count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a> and Ribagorza. After his death Ribagorza went to one of his sons.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a mountain society of herders and farmers, defended by castles against the Muslim lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbastro">Barbastro</a>. They founded a bishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roda-de-isabena">Roda de Isábena</a>, whose cathedral was consecrated in 956, making it the county's religious centre. The county shared its culture and language with both Aragon and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor's</a> raids weakened the county around 1000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a> took control in the 1010s and 1020s, and in 1035 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> passed with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sobrarbe">Sobrarbe</a> to his son Gonzalo. After Gonzalo's death around 1045 it went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-aragon">Ramiro I</a>, joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-ribagorza.jpg" alt="County of Ribagorza" width="320" height="121"><figcaption>Image: Miguillen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reino_de_Pamplona_Sancho_III.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>872 CE – 1044 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Roda de Isábena</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ramon I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ribagorça</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=958&amp;focus=county-of-ribagorza" aria-label="County of Ribagorza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4693 673 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Ribagorza lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5013 929L5002 924M5013 929L5035 888M5013 929L4960 913M5013 929L4979 949M5013 929L5027 925"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5002 924h0M5035 888h0M4960 913h0M4979 949h0M5027 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5013 929h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5013 929h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Roda de Isábena. <a href="/atlas?year=958&amp;focus=county-of-ribagorza">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
